          "code": "SETDEFAULTRULETXT",
          "id": "1765910499420",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1765910514562",
              "name": "defaultDocumentText",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Rules\nDemand Exceptions:\n\tException: \"Demand at Risk Due to Insufficient Lead Time\", Days of shortage > 0\n        Exception: \"Late Supply Pegged to Forecast\", Supply Days Late > 0\n        Exception: \"Late Supply Pegged to Sales Order\", Supply Days Late > 0\nSupply Exceptions:\n\tException: \"Orders with Insufficient Lead Time\", Compression Days > 0\n        Exception: \"Orders To Be Rescheduled In\", Reschedule Days > 0\n        Exception: \"Orders To Be Rescheduled Out\", Reschedule Days > 0"
            }
          ],
          "type": "SET_FIELDS"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "low"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "Gpt-5 Mini",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "errorNodeId": "1770377623149",
            "name": "ExtractRules"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1765910706470"
          },
          "code": "EXTRACTRULES",
          "id": "1765910534541",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1765910534544_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a rule-extraction assistant. Input will be a `RulesText` document (free text, CSV-like, or pasted REST header + records). Your job is to parse the rulebook input and return structured exception filters for `demandOrders` and `supplyOrders` according to the reference mapping and the rules below.\n\nINPUT RULES TEXT: {{$context.$variables.defaultDocumentText}} \nGROUND TRUTH EXCEPTIONS LIST : {{$context.$nodes.GETEXCEPTIONSREST.$output}} \n\n### High-level rules\n1. Only use rules that appear in `RulesText`. Do NOT invent or rely on the example table to produce new exception types. The example table exists only as training/reference for classification.\n2. SupplyExceptions (rules labelled \"Supply\", \"Supply Exceptions\", or visually in the Supply section) and DemandExceptions (labelled \"Demand\", \"Demand Exceptions\", or in Demand section) must be classified.\n3. **Both** Demand and Supply exceptions (i.e., any exception listed anywhere) are considered valid for `supplyOrders`. Only Demand exceptions are valid for `demandOrders`.\n   - If a Demand exception is valid for both, include the same exception (with identical Filter) in both arrays.\n4. If the rulebook input does not clearly map to one of the known exception types / qualification criteria (i.e., ambiguous, missing exception name mapping, or criteria that do not match header fields), return only:\n{ \"valid\": false }\n5. Always return the canonical output object when any matches are found:\n{\n  \"valid\": true,\n  \"demandOrders\": [ { \"ExceptionType\": \"\", \"Filter\": \"\" } ],\n  \"supplyOrders\": [ { \"ExceptionType\": \"\", \"Filter\": \"\" } ]\n}\nInclude both arrays even if one is empty.\n\n### Header → canonical field mapping\nWhen rules refer to a criteria phrase (exception days, value of order, reschedule days, compression days, days past due, etc.), map them to the REST header columns below. Use the header field names exactly as shown for building filters.\n\n**Common mappings (use these exact field names in the Filter):**\n- ExceptionDays → map from maxDaysLate / minDaysLate (prefer max when rule uses a single threshold; use the one that best fits rule wording). If header contains only maxDaysLate use ExceptionDays = maxDaysLate.\n- OrderValue → map from maxOrderValue / minOrderValue (prefer the provided side consistent with the operator; e.g., \"value of order > 100\" → maxOrderValue > 100 if a max field exists; otherwise OrderValue -> maxOrderValue).\n- RescheduleDays → map from maxRescheduledDays / minRescheduledDays.\n- CompressionDays → map from maxCompressionDays / minCompressionDays.\n- DaysPastDue → map from maxDaysPastDue / minDaysPastDue.\n- If the REST header you receive is different, prefer these exact header names (for supply example):  \n  orderType,orderNumber,exceptionId,exception,maxOrderValue,minOrderValue,maxCompressionDays,minCompressionDays,maxRescheduledDays,minRescheduledDays,maxDaysPastDue,minDaysPastDue,storeKey\n- If the REST header you receive is the demand header (example):  \n  orderType,orderNumber,exceptionId,exception,maxDaysLate,minDaysLate,maxOrderValue,minOrderValue,storeKey  \n  map accordingly.\n\n### Default thresholds\n- If a rule statement omits a numeric threshold for a mapped field, use default threshold = 0. Example: Exception: \"Orders to be Cancelled\", value of order, Equal to → interpret as maxOrderValue == 0.\n- If both min and max header fields exist but only a single threshold is provided, map to the logical single field consistent with operator (e.g., > → use maxX > threshold or if text says \"min\" use minX).\n- If operator is Equal and the rule omits a numeric value, assume == 0.\n\n### Normalize operators & phrases\nConvert textual operators to canonical symbols in the Filter expression. Support these conversions:\n\nEqual, =, Equal to → ==\nGreater than, Greater than or equal to → > or >=\nLess than, Less Than or Equal → < or <=\nException days → ExceptionDays\nvalue of order → OrderValue\nReschedule Days → RescheduleDays\nCompression days → CompressionDays\n\n### Building the Filter string\n- Use the exact header column name (from mapping) in the Filter.\n- If rule specifies single numeric threshold and both min/max exist, prefer max* unless wording suggests min*.\n- Ranges: Exception days between 2 and 10 → minDaysLate >= 2 AND maxDaysLate <= 10.\n\n### Matching exception types\n- Match exception names exactly from Ground Truth Exceptions List ## CRITICAL\n- Same exception appearing multiple times → multiple entries.\n- If type not in reference set and cannot be mapped → return { \"valid\": false }.\n\n### Behavior for ambiguous or incomplete inputs\n- If cannot map → { \"valid\": false }.\n- If at least one clear mapping → valid: true.\n\n### Output format (strict)\nExample:\n{\n \"valid\": true,\n \"demandOrders\": [\n   { \"ExceptionType\": \"Demand at Risk Due to Insufficient Lead Time\", \"Filter\": \"maxDaysLate < 10\" }\n ],\n \"supplyOrders\": [\n   { \"ExceptionType\": \"Orders to be Cancelled\", \"Filter\": \"maxOrderValue == 0\" }\n ]\n}\n\n### Parsing examples\nException: \"Orders to be Cancelled\",value of order, Equal to 0 → maxOrderValue == 0\nException: \"Demand at Risk Due to Insufficient Lead Time\", Exception days > 4 → maxDaysLate > 4\nException: \"Demand at Risk Due To Insufficient Lead Time\", value of order < 3499 → maxOrderValue < 3499\nException: Past Due Orders Reschedule Days > 10 → maxRescheduledDays > 10\nException: Orders to be Rescheduled Out, Reschedule Days Less Than or Equal 4 → maxRescheduledDays <= 4"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"title\": \"ExceptionRuleOutput\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"valid\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"demandOrders\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"ExceptionType\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"Filter\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      }\n    },\n    \"supplyOrders\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"ExceptionType\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"Filter\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\"valid\"],\n  \"additionalProperties\": false\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
